accessWidener v2 official

# `official` rather than `named`, and there is no longer a difference: Minecraft is unobfuscated from 26.1,
# so there is one namespace and Loom expects it to be called this. It says so plainly if it is not —
# "Expected official namespace for access widener entry, found: named".

# So a level can be built after the server has started.
#
# **These four are the whole toll for runtime dimensions**, and they are the ingredients of vanilla's own
# `createLevels` loop rather than a novel liberty: it builds a secondary level from exactly
# `(executor, storageSource, DerivedLevelData(worldData, …), dimension, stem)` and puts it in `levels`. We do
# the same thing later, which is the only difference.
#
# **A widener rather than a Mixin, deliberately.** The alternative is an accessor Mixin per field, four more
# entries in the Mixin config, and the same access granted less legibly. Nothing is overridden and no
# behaviour is replaced — these are reads of state the server already keeps.
#
# **Do not reach for the NeoForge equivalents.** The patched jar carries `forgeGetWorldMap()` and
# `markWorldsDirty()`, and `common` compiles against *vanilla* (NeoForm), where neither exists — code written
# against them compiles and then dies on Fabric. See `notes/neoforge-dimensions-research.md`.
accessible	field	net/minecraft/server/MinecraftServer	levels	Ljava/util/Map;
accessible	field	net/minecraft/server/MinecraftServer	executor	Ljava/util/concurrent/Executor;
accessible	field	net/minecraft/server/MinecraftServer	storageSource	Lnet/minecraft/world/level/storage/LevelStorageSource$LevelStorageAccess;
accessible	field	net/minecraft/server/MinecraftServer	worldData	Lnet/minecraft/world/level/storage/WorldData;
